    <h1>About China AI Hub</h1>

    <p>
      China AI Hub is an independent English-language knowledge and intelligence platform for
      understanding China's AI models, agents, APIs, companies, technologies, pricing and ecosystem.
      It exists to answer one practical question: <em>which Chinese AI model, agent or API is suitable
      for which task, at what cost, with what capabilities, and under what deployment conditions?</em>
    </p>

    <h2>Why this website exists</h2>
    <p>
      China's AI ecosystem moves quickly and its documentation is fragmented across Chinese-language
      sources. International developers and researchers routinely lack a reliable, structured, English-language
      view of what exists, what it costs, and what evidence supports it. China AI Hub fills that gap with
      structured data and sourced analysis — not with hype or rankings.
    </p>

    <h2>What China AI Hub is</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A <strong>database</strong> of models, companies, agents, APIs, pricing and benchmarks</li>
      <li>A <strong>knowledge base</strong> of AI technologies and their Chinese implementations</li>
      <li>A <strong>comparison system</strong> that explains trade-offs, not winners</li>
      <li>A <strong>research platform</strong> publishing original analysis with clearly labeled evidence</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What China AI Hub is not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A news aggregator or translation site</li>
      <li>A "Top 10 Chinese AI models" blog</li>
      <li>A ranking site that declares one best model</li>
      <li>A collection of rewritten official documentation</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who it serves</h2>
    <p>
      AI developers, software engineers, researchers, enterprise technology buyers, startups,
      analysts and journalists — anyone evaluating Chinese AI systems from outside China.
      Content assumes an intelligent reader who knows AI but may not know China's AI ecosystem.
    </p>

    <h2>How data is sourced</h2>
    <p>
      Every important factual claim carries a source, a source type, a publication date and a
      last-verified date. Primary sources (official documentation, model cards, pricing pages,
      papers, GitHub) are preferred; community sources never carry critical facts alone.
      Where sources conflict, the conflict is explained, not silently resolved.
      Where data is unknown, the site says so. Nothing is invented.
    </p>

    <h2>Analysis policy</h2>
    <p>
      Editorial analysis (including the China AI Hub View sections) is always labeled as analysis
      and is traceable to cited evidence. The site does not promote or attack companies, does not
      declare universal winners, and does not pretend certainty where none exists.
    </p>
  </article>
